OpenBSD's nvme(4) hardcodes 128-entry queues and never reads CAP.MQES,
the queue size limit the controller advertises. Nitro EBS controllers
advertise only ~32 entries (measured on Graviton), which breaks the
driver in three places, all addressed here:

1. nvme_attach creates the I/O queue with 128 entries; the controller
   rejects ADD_IOCQ with "Invalid Queue Size" (SCT 1, SC 0x02) and the
   disk never attaches ("nvme0: unable to create io q"). The queue is
   sized to MIN(128, NVME_CAP_MQES(cap)) instead.

2. With the queue that small, sc_openings = 64 becomes inconsistent:
   the SCSI layer is told it may keep 64 commands in flight, but the
   submission ring holds at most q_entries - 1 and nvme_q_submit never
   checks for a full ring - the tail laps the head, commands get
   completed twice and the kernel faults on poisoned scsi_xfer
   pointers (seen on a t4g.medium during library reordering at boot).
   sc_openings is clamped to q_entries - 1; saa.saa_openings picks up
   the clamped value.

3. nvme_resume re-creates the I/O queue from scratch with the same
   hardcoded 128, so the first suspend/resume cycle would hit the
   attach-time failure again and lose the disk. Same clamp there,
   with CAP re-read before the allocation.

The admin queue stays at 128 on purpose: CAP.MQES applies to the I/O
queues the host creates, while the admin queue is sized via the AQA
register with its own 4096-entry ceiling, and Nitro accepts 128 there.
NetBSD's descendant of this driver likewise clamps only the I/O queues.

--- sys/dev/ic/nvme.c
+++ sys/dev/ic/nvme.c
@@ -374,7 +374,8 @@
 	}
 	nccbs = 64;
 
-	sc->sc_q = nvme_q_alloc(sc, NVME_IO_Q, 128, sc->sc_dstrd);
+	sc->sc_q = nvme_q_alloc(sc, NVME_IO_Q, MIN(128, NVME_CAP_MQES(cap)),
+	    sc->sc_dstrd);
 	if (sc->sc_q == NULL) {
 		printf("%s: unable to allocate io q\n", DEVNAME(sc));
 		goto disable;
@@ -385,6 +386,10 @@
 		goto free_q;
 	}
 
+	/* the submission ring holds at most q_entries - 1 commands */
+	if (sc->sc_openings > sc->sc_q->q_entries - 1)
+		sc->sc_openings = sc->sc_q->q_entries - 1;
+
 #ifdef HIBERNATE
 	sc->sc_hib_q = nvme_q_alloc(sc, NVME_HIB_Q, 4, sc->sc_dstrd);
 	if (sc->sc_hib_q == NULL) {
@@ -457,6 +462,8 @@
 int
 nvme_resume(struct nvme_softc *sc)
 {
+	u_int64_t cap;
+
 	if (nvme_disable(sc) != 0) {
 		printf("%s: unable to disable controller\n", DEVNAME(sc));
 		return (1);
@@ -472,7 +479,9 @@
 		return (1);
 	}
 
-	sc->sc_q = nvme_q_alloc(sc, NVME_IO_Q, 128, sc->sc_dstrd);
+	cap = nvme_read8(sc, NVME_CAP);
+	sc->sc_q = nvme_q_alloc(sc, NVME_IO_Q, MIN(128, NVME_CAP_MQES(cap)),
+	    sc->sc_dstrd);
 	if (sc->sc_q == NULL) {
 		printf("%s: unable to allocate io q\n", DEVNAME(sc));
 		goto disable;
